        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "90b2089a-ac0d-5c07-811d-f887682f2625",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Kelvin Sampson, Tony Crocker, Alden Pasche, Jeff Capel III\n- Organizations: Houston Cougars men's basketball team, University of Oklahoma, Indiana University, Washington State University, Montana Tech, American Athletic Conference\n- Events: 2014–15 Houston Cougars men's basketball season, 2015 American Conference Tournament\n- Roles: Head coaches\n\nFacts:\n- Alden Pasche was born on July 19, 1910, and he later became the first head coach of the Houston Cougars men's basketball team.\n- Alden Pasche served as the first head coach of the Houston Cougars men's basketball team from 1946 to 1956.\n- Kelvin Sampson was born on October 5, 1955.\n- Kelvin Sampson was the head coach at Montana Tech from 1981 to 1985.\n- Kelvin Sampson was the head coach at Washington State University from 1987 to 1994.\n- Kelvin Sampson was the head coach at the University of Oklahoma from 1994 to 2006.\n- Kelvin Sampson was the head coach at Indiana University from 2006 to 2008.\n- Tony Crocker signed a letter of intent to play college basketball at the University of Oklahoma under Kelvin Sampson.\n- Tony Crocker kept his commitment to the University of Oklahoma after Kelvin Sampson left for Indiana University and Jeff Capel III became the Sooners' new coach.\n- The 2014–15 season was Houston's second season as a member of the American Athletic Conference and its first season under head coach Kelvin Sampson.\n- The 2014–15 Houston Cougars men's basketball team finished with a 13–19 overall record and a 4–14 conference record, placing tenth in the American Athletic Conference.\n- The 2014–15 Houston Cougars men's basketball team reached the quarterfinals of the 2015 American Conference Tournament, where Tulsa defeated them.\n- Kelvin Sampson is an American basketball coach who is currently the head coach of the Houston Cougars men's basketball team.\n- Alden Pasche died on May 9, 1986.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "15e1fe14-c5d6-5cfa-81f6-5de31ff9e8d2",
        "text": "Page: 2014–15 Houston Cougars men's basketball team. Sentence: It was their first season under head coach Kelvin Sampson and second as members of the American Athletic Conference.\nPage: 2014–15 Houston Cougars men's basketball team. Sentence: Their record was 13–19, and 4–14 in conference play to finish in tenth place.\nPage: 2014–15 Houston Cougars men's basketball team. Sentence: They advanced to the quarterfinals of the 2015 American Conference Tournament, where they lost to Tulsa.\nPage: Kelvin Sampson. Sentence: Kelvin Matthew Sampson (born October 5, 1955) is an American basketball coach who is currently the head coach of the Houston Cougars men's basketball team.\nPage: Kelvin Sampson. Sentence: He was a head coach at Montana Tech from 1981 to 1985, Washington State University from 1987 to 1994, the University of Oklahoma from 1994 to 2006, and Indiana University 2006 to 2008.\nPage: Tony Crocker. Sentence: Crocker signed a letter of intent to play college basketball at the University of Oklahoma under Kelvin Sampson, and stuck to his commitment when coach Sampson left to Indiana University and coach Jeff Capel III became the new coach of the Sooners.\nPage: Alden Pasche. Sentence: Herbert Alden Pasche (July 19, 1910 – May 9, 1986) was the first head coach of the Houston Cougars men's basketball team from 1946 to 1956.",
